INT. AMBULANCE BAY - RURAL HOSPITAL - NIGHT

Sodium lights, wet pavement. The bay doors yawn open to a rainy road. Gurneys and oxygen tanks line the wall. A radio on a shelf murmurs static.

DR. AMARA NWOSU (40s, ER attending, damp scrubs) paces. NURSE BENEDICT "BEN" HOLLOWAY (30s, steady hands) checks a crash cart. PARAMEDIC FIONA GALLAGHER (30s, reflective jacket) stands by the doors, watching the road.

Ben spots lights on the wet road. He nods at Fiona.

BEN
They're here.

An ambulance backs in, rear doors swinging. A gurney comes out fast. On it lies MARCEL DUPRE (50s, farmer, soaked flannel), pale and sweating.

A radio on Fiona's shoulder crackles.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Unit Four, status on your patient? Over.

FIONA
(into radio)
Unit Four. Arrived. Transferring now.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Copy, Four.

AMARA
Fiona, what do you have?

FIONA
Fifty-one-year-old male, chest pain, diaphoretic. BP eighty over fifty. Nitro given. No relief.

AMARA
Ben, get a twelve-lead.

BEN
On it, Doctor.

He attaches the leads in four fast movements. The monitor shows a ragged line.

BEN (CONT'D)
He's in V-fib.

AMARA
Charge to two hundred.

BEN
Charging.

AMARA
Everybody clear.

FIONA ^
Everybody clear.

The shock lifts Marcel's back off the gurney. The monitor squeals. Flat.

AMARA
Again.

BEN
Charging. Three hundred.

AMARA
Clear!

Another shock. Marcel convulses. The monitor shows nothing. Then a blip. Another. The line settles into a ragged rhythm.

BEN
We have a pulse.

AMARA
Ben, breathe.

BEN
I am breathing.

AMARA
Then stop saying it out loud.

Fiona, hands on her knees, lets out a short laugh. Ben wipes his forehead. The radio crackles again.

DISPATCH (V.O.)
Unit Four, we have another call, motor vehicle collision, route nine. Can you respond? Over.

Fiona looks at Amara. Amara looks at Fiona. Neither says anything. Rain drums on the bay roof.

FIONA
(into radio)
Dispatch, Unit Four. Responding.

AMARA
Fiona, you've been up thirty hours.

FIONA
Thirty-one. Who's counting?

AMARA
Ben is. Aren't you, Ben?

BEN
Thirty-two, actually.

Fiona grins, tired. She grabs her kit and jogs for the ambulance. The doors slam. The siren wails. The headlights swing across the bay and are gone.

Amara turns to the gurney. Marcel's eyes flutter open. He grips her sleeve.

MARCEL
(faint)
I'm sorry for the trouble.

AMARA
No trouble, Mr. Dupre.

She nods to Ben. Together they wheel him inside.
